                <pb facs="0831" n="737" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg737"/>

                <head>No. 479.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Manning to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-06-29"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 29, 1887</date>.
                        (Received July 7.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 107.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your No. 100, of May 6,
                    touching the complaint of Messrs. Pomares &amp; Cushman, of New York, of
                    vexatious and obstructive fines imposed upon them by Mexican customs officers
                    for trifling and <pb facs="0832" n="738" xml:id="pg738"/> unimportant omissions
                    in their invoices, and to my note to Mr. Mariscal, of May19, I have now the
                    honor to inclose translation of his reply, and copy of my rejoinder of
                    yesterday’s date.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Th. C. Manning.</hi></signed></closer>




